#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107041 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss some fundamental objects in geometry. The objects we'll discuss are some of the building blocks we use to describe all geometrical objects.\n\nA **point** is an exact location in space. It has no size (i.e., no length, width, or depth), only position. A point is indicated with a dot usually labeled with a capital letter (e.g., $P$, $Q$, $S$,...), as shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/aceb9052aee93f2d7ef5cfc3f6a9c7ace291e6c9525751a2d826c0fbfa0b36ed.png)\n\nA **line** is a straight object that is infinitely long and has no width. We can think of it as an infinite collection of points, extending infinitely in both directions. A line has no endpoints!\n\nLet's take a look at the following line.\n\n![](images/86/861fbcc84e4e293379616a5e18d9073ecfdf4af5f6d17dece22196d332b62899.png)\n\nA line that passes through the points $P$ and $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longleftrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **both** directions.\n\nLines can also be indicated with lowercase letters. For example:\n\n![](images/0a/0a2d566ad6126527f4b1a2b5a992688a668b9ff901c6b7716381521a576a0594.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a6361854-d380-59ab-8f5c-a64142f383c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2161 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107135 :tutorial/content "A **ray** is similar to a line but has a starting point and extends infinitely in one direction only. An example of a ray is shown below.\n\n![](images/75/750aef546281b34fef8e9aef61c1260894128138022e971d6c19dfd834cfa1e2.png)\n\nA ray that starts at the point $P$ and passes through a point $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **one** direction. Notice that the arrow's tail is above the starting point $P$.\n\nSimilar to lines, rays have no width but are infinite in length." :tutorial/id #uuid "74865d26-a257-5d2d-a7cd-537189af9954" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6647 :tutorial/title "Rays"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107136 :tutorial/content "A **segment** is part of a line bounded by two points, called the **endpoints** of the segment. An example of a segment is shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0d8c12318cc57412be73f2cfb077efa5b06472554e75be41af233c87a9ab17.png)\n\nA segment with endpoints $A$ and $B$ is denoted $\\overline{AB}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee97c2fb-0c77-5af6-b979-587c04a1f0c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6648 :tutorial/title "Segments"}]}
